WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.550
Dr. Kathleen O'Toole: I'd like to introduce
now our keynote speaker.

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:11.170
Let me begin by saying the following: it makes
absolutely no sense that someone like Patrick

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:16.610
Sajak is here at our graduation, except that
Patrick Sajak is a great man.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:17.610
He is not here because he is a famous man.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.610
He is here because he is a great man.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:19.920
Let me tell you about his life.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.980
He was born in Chicago.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:33.480
He went to Columbia College, where he began
a broadcasting career as a radio announcer

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.480
in 1967.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.970
In 1968 he joined the Army.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.880
He was deployed to Vietnam, where he was the
morning D.J.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.620
on the Armed Forces Radio firm Saigon.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:52.240
He opened the broadcast by saying "Good morning,
Vietnam."

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.750
Following his military service, his career
took him to Lousiville, Kentucky, Nashville,

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:03.520
Tennessee, and finally, in 1977, to L.A.,
where he was a TV weatherman and talk show

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:04.520
host.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:10.610
He's been the host of Wheel of Fortune since
1981, and like many of you, probably, I grew

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.210
up watching him on TV.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:18.049
He's earned three Emmy Awards, a People's
Choice Award, and a star on the Hollywood

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:20.080
Walk of Fame.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:28.810
I've always been struck by how kind Mr. Sajak
is to the people on the show, how personal

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:35.860
an interest he pays to each of them, and how
he is always good-humored, always gentlemanly,

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.740
always welcoming, and as I've gotten to know
him a little bit, I see that that is not a

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:41.740
front.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:42.740
He's actually like that.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:50.020
The way that he is on TV is the way that he
is, as a person.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.909
That is impressive.

00:01:51.909 --> 00:02:01.420
To our students, and to all of us, Pat Sajak
is an inspiration because he has lived a life

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.520
of fame, a successful career in the public
eye, and he has always kept the important

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.459
things first.

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:13.459
He is an example of virtue and of devotion
to his country.

00:02:13.459 --> 00:02:19.370
He is an excellent citizen, and he understands
the importance of education.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.969
We are grateful to have him here today.

00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:34.330
Please join me in welcoming Mr. Pat Sajak.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:40.779
PAT SAJAK: Thank you, Dr. O'Toole.

00:02:40.779 --> 00:02:46.819
This being a solemn and serious occasion,
let me begin by saying, wow, this is cool,

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:47.819
isn't it?

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:50.650
I mean, it really is.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.189
I'm very excited to be here.

00:02:52.189 --> 00:02:56.790
I do want to make one thing clear, because
frequently, when I appear somewhere, there

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.310
are some misunderstandings.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:00.909
And people are expecting something else.

00:03:00.909 --> 00:03:05.510
So if you came for the wrong reason, I just
want to announce, I am not giving away cars

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.150
or cash today.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:13.980
[LAUGHTER]
Being introduced by Dr. O'Toole is a strange

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:14.980
experience for me.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:18.709
I've known the Arnn family for something like
25 years.

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:24.790
And this will shock some of you in the room,
particularly students, to know that Dr. O'Toole

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.620
was not always Dr. O'Toole.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.199
She was little Katie O'Toole.

00:03:28.199 --> 00:03:32.010
No, Katie Arnn, I'm sorry.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.790
I'm so confused.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:40.989
And I'm so proud and in awe of what she, with
the help, of course, of a wonderful staff

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:45.420
and the faculty here, and the parents, and
the students, what they've accomplished in

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.650
these few years.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:52.329
So Dr. O'Toole, and Dr. Arnn, and Mr. Terry,
and to the faculty, and the parents and families,

00:03:52.329 --> 00:03:56.879
and the members of the class of 2017, it really
is an honor to be here.

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:01.620
I flew in yesterday and came directly to the
school and took a tour of about an hour and

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:02.620
a half.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.560
And I met a lot of you and saw a lot of the
classes.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.859
I went into a kindergarten class, which, like
most kindergarten classes everywhere, was

00:04:10.859 --> 00:04:19.780
well-behaved, respectful, quiet, and like
most kindergarten class everywhere, recited,

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.889
in unison, the preamble to the Constitution.

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:30.610
I then went to a second grade class, where
they proceeded to recite, in unison, the Gettysburg

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.120
Address.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:37.270
When I was in second grade, I was learning
to memorize Bah Bah Black Sheep and learning

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:41.479
to tie my shoes.

00:04:41.479 --> 00:04:46.759
So to the graduating class who've been working
so hard the last three years at a classical

00:04:46.759 --> 00:04:51.270
education, I suspect it's sometimes easy to
lose sight of what a magnificent place this

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:52.270
is.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:57.240
But from the bottom of my heart, I tell you
how in awe of all of you I am and how much

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.830
I congratulate you all for what you've accomplished
here.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.300
It's really amazing.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:07.950
I live and work in Hollywood, and I also live
much of the year near Washington, DC.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:14.479
And in both places, the idea of a school such
as Founders is not very popular.

00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:18.710
My show business colleagues in the west and
my political friends in the east are very

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:23.449
supportive of a more typical public school.

00:05:23.449 --> 00:05:29.680
They're supportive only to the point of not
sending their children there.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.909
You might call that hypocritical, but I certainly
wouldn't do that.

00:05:32.909 --> 00:05:35.939
[LAUGHTER]
I've been doing Wheel of Fortune since before

00:05:35.939 --> 00:05:41.180
all of you students were born, before most
of the faculty was born, perhaps before many

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.090
of these buildings were built.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.360
[LAUGHTER]
And in that time, I've become used to the

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.580
strange phenomenon of celebrityhood.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:53.470
And one of the things I've learned is not
to take it too seriously.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:59.190
Back in the 1950s, when The Today Show was
just getting started on NBC, it was hosted

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.039
by a man named Dave Garroway.

00:06:01.039 --> 00:06:08.330
And a regular on the show, he was there every
day, was a chimpanzee named J. Fred Muggs.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:15.260
And J. Fred wore a suit and sat there, and
mugged and smiled, and did all the things

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.190
chimpanzees do.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:18.479
And he was hugely popular.

00:06:18.479 --> 00:06:19.650
He was on magazine covers.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:22.380
He received thousands of fan mails.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.870
People loved J. Fred Muggs.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:29.319
So I figure, if a chimpanzee can become a
celebrity, there's no need for me to take

00:06:29.319 --> 00:06:32.080
it too seriously when I go through life.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.039
I had another lesson on that a few years ago.

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:39.530
I was at a Washington Nationals baseball game
with my son.

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:41.000
And the game was over.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.740
We were sitting about three rows up from the
dugout.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.879
And I said, why don't we wait, let the crowd
clear out?

00:06:46.879 --> 00:06:50.560
And as we were standing there, I noticed,
as you do-- you learn to notice peripherally

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.460
when someone's coming at you to ask you for
an autograph or a picture or a handshake or

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:55.460
whatever.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.830
But there was a group of young girls coming
over.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:02.069
And sure enough, they asked if I could take
a picture, and I said sure.

00:07:02.069 --> 00:07:06.259
And normally, when someone asks that, they
get next to you and then hand the camera to

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:07.259
someone.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:10.639
In this case, they actually went to the row
in front of me looking up.

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:13.860
And I'm thinking, you know, they're very young
and I'm old.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.789
I don't want to crawl over these seats down
here.

00:07:15.789 --> 00:07:19.960
So there was an awkward moment while we were
all trying to figure out how to handle the

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:24.499
situation, when my son leaned over to me and
said, dad, they want you to take their picture.

00:07:24.499 --> 00:07:31.479
They had no idea.

00:07:31.479 --> 00:07:35.199
One must be humble.

00:07:35.199 --> 00:07:36.620
So I've gotten used to celebrityhood.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.520
I've also gotten used to being on television
in front of millions of people.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.610
I don't think much about it now.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.840
But that's not true of most of our players.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:51.740
And as a result, sometimes, they, in nervousness,
will say silly things.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.729
We had one case, and this had nothing to do
with nerves.

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:55.889
This was something else.

00:07:55.889 --> 00:08:00.889
And this would not have happened if this player
had been educated in this place.

00:08:00.889 --> 00:08:02.979
It was during a college week, of all things.

00:08:02.979 --> 00:08:09.000
And a young man, a college junior, as I recall,
was working on the puzzle, which was as follows--

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.969
mythological hero Achilles.

00:08:12.969 --> 00:08:14.379
And he was struggling.

00:08:14.379 --> 00:08:16.729
And all the letters got up there.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:19.810
So all he had to do was read it.

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:25.999
He said, and I quote, "mythological hero 'a-shill-eyes.'"

00:08:25.999 --> 00:08:30.930
[LAUGHTER]
So obviously, we couldn't accept that.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:34.010
And look, I'm not making fun because people
make mistakes.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:36.590
But what was rather appalling was his attitude
about it.

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:37.849
He was pretty unapologetic.

00:08:37.849 --> 00:08:43.070
And as we went to commercial, he leaned over
to me and said, well, I'm a business major,

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:45.060
as if that excused things.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:50.089
And that speaks to a growing idea in this
country that secondary and higher education

00:08:50.089 --> 00:08:57.019
is about job training and not about learning
the things that make us better people and

00:08:57.019 --> 00:08:58.019
better citizens.

00:08:58.019 --> 00:09:03.149
These graduates here chose a different path,
a path that is certainly more difficult, certainly

00:09:03.149 --> 00:09:07.860
more challenging, but as you've heard from
them today, certainly more rewarding.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.480
They chose a path that recognizes the need
for an education that includes, as the mission

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.220
statement here says, instruction in good character
and civic virtue.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:21.570
And when I'm not selling vowels, as you heard,
I'm the Vice Chairman of the Board of Trustees

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:22.810
of Hillsdale College.

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:27.949
And putting my family aside, there's nothing
in my life of which I'm more proud.

00:09:27.949 --> 00:09:33.410
I was there as plans evolved for Hillsdale's
Barney Charter School Initiative.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:37.500
I've had the opportunity to work with Steve
Barney, an amazingly dedicated and generous

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:44.089
man who feels strongly about the direction
of education and has forcefully and successfully

00:09:44.089 --> 00:09:46.850
begun to do something about it.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:50.870
We were just at the spring commencement meeting,
the board meeting.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:57.920
And you should know that every board member,
every member of the staff, faculty at Hillsdale,

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.980
and some of whom you heard are here today,
really salutes what you've done and is behind

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:03.980
you.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:07.830
And they are so excited about this first graduating
class.

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:10.709
When you're asked to give a commencement address,
the first thing you think about is, what can

00:10:10.709 --> 00:10:14.759
you possibly tell these young men and women
that they don't already know?

00:10:14.759 --> 00:10:19.520
Especially here at Founders, where simply
because you chose to be educated in this particular

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:22.259
way, I already know a great deal about them.

00:10:22.259 --> 00:10:25.370
I have a sense of their ambition and of their
standards.

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:29.149
I have a pretty good idea of where their moral
compass points.

00:10:29.149 --> 00:10:31.320
And I know they're not afraid of challenges.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:34.570
I know they have people in their lives who
care deeply about them.

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:38.839
And I know that their futures will be filled
with accomplishments and good works.

00:10:38.839 --> 00:10:43.190
In short, I'm not sure there's much need to
try to inspire a group like this.

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:44.750
They have already been inspired.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:47.920
And what they've accomplished is inspiring.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:53.610
So let me instead suggest a pitfall to avoid
going forward, a pitfall not only for recent

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:57.350
graduates, but for everyone in every corner
of American society.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:01.550
It has altered the way we talk to one another,
the way we perceive one another.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:05.519
It has perverted the notion of free speech,
poisoned the academic environment.

00:11:05.519 --> 00:11:10.470
It has turned the American political system
on its head, creating a situation where opposing

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:15.000
views are not only unwelcomed, they are deemed
to be signs of evil intent.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.009
It has pitted friend against friend, coworker
against coworker, and has even caused rifts

00:11:20.009 --> 00:11:21.459
within families.

00:11:21.459 --> 00:11:27.240
I'm talking about identity politics, the attempt
not to unite us as Americans, but to divide

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.730
us and set us against each other.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:34.509
The attempt to classify and categorize us
by all sorts of measurements and standards.

00:11:34.509 --> 00:11:40.190
The attempt to subvert the values of some
by proclaiming a new, superior set of values.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:44.260
And to a great degree, those who are making
these attempts are succeeding.

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:48.010
And their efforts are changing our country
in fundamental ways.

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:49.829
We are all members of groups.

00:11:49.829 --> 00:11:51.250
I'm of Polish descent.

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:56.870
I'm a baby boomer, one of those tens of millions
born in the aftermath of World War II.

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:57.870
I'm a male.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:58.870
I'm Caucasian.

00:11:58.870 --> 00:11:59.870
A proud Caucasian, by the way.

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:05.110
But while all of these facts helped shape
me to varying degrees, I am mostly Pat Sajak.

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:10.709
I am a unique individual, created by God and
blessed to be born in a country whose founders

00:12:10.709 --> 00:12:16.400
believed that that very creation endowed me
with unalienable rights-- among them, life,

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:18.130
liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:21.250
I grew up in an industrial area of Chicago.

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:22.950
My parents were divorced.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:27.360
We often struggled to make ends meet, but
our struggles were no greater than millions

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:28.360
of others.

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:31.450
And I always believed that I could and I would
overcome them.

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:36.149
It never occurred to me that I was the hopeless
victim of a system or that the success of

00:12:36.149 --> 00:12:39.620
others was inhibiting my ability to succeed.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:43.750
Envy and anger and resentment, these were
wastes of time and energy.

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:48.639
I always believed that an individual by the
name of Pat Sajak would make something of

00:12:48.639 --> 00:12:49.670
himself.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:52.570
And I don't mean becoming a TV personality.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:57.839
I mean improving my life, making a difference
in the lives of others, and growing up to

00:12:57.839 --> 00:13:01.130
become a contributing citizen of this great
country.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:05.750
Today, this graduating class is made up of
11 individuals who have worked hard to be

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:07.649
in this place and in this situation.

00:13:07.649 --> 00:13:09.959
They are from various backgrounds.

00:13:09.959 --> 00:13:13.779
Each of them has to deal with a unique set
of challenges.

00:13:13.779 --> 00:13:14.990
But they did it.

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:19.870
They were helped by parents, educators, friends,
supporters of the school, and a whole host

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:20.870
of others.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:22.130
But you did it.

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:26.529
As you move on, however, you will find a world
all too eager to diminish your accomplishment

00:13:26.529 --> 00:13:28.920
and minimize your individuality.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:33.040
Some of you will find yourselves in colleges
that will try to define you and separate you

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:36.860
by race, religion, politics, social attitudes.

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:42.291
You'll be exposed to the notion that the truths
you learned here at Founders are, well, sort

00:13:42.291 --> 00:13:47.209
of truths, subject to prevailing winds; that
good character and civic virtue are flexible

00:13:47.209 --> 00:13:50.569
terms, open to interpretation and alteration.

00:13:50.569 --> 00:13:55.270
You'll be courted by interests trying to establish
an us-against-them mentality.

00:13:55.270 --> 00:14:00.709
Some of you will encounter disdain because
you allegedly benefited from so-called privileges,

00:14:00.709 --> 00:14:04.440
while others will preach to you about your
victimhood and how you must now separate yourselves

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.699
from the forces that held you back.

00:14:06.699 --> 00:14:10.120
You'll be approached by political organizations
who will try to convince you that, because

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:15.269
of the demographic groups to which you belong,
defined by age or race or gender, that you

00:14:15.269 --> 00:14:20.899
must think and act and vote in a particular
way, and that those who don't share a defined

00:14:20.899 --> 00:14:24.910
set of views and attitudes are automatically
evil and malicious.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:28.540
Just as they try to diminish your individuality,
they will try to discourage you from looking

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:31.009
at others as individuals.

00:14:31.009 --> 00:14:37.529
The media is complicit in this effort to label
things-- sometimes entire generations.

00:14:37.529 --> 00:14:38.569
They love labeling folks.

00:14:38.569 --> 00:14:41.139
As I've said, I'm a baby boomer, according
to them.

00:14:41.139 --> 00:14:44.019
And you've heard a lot about millennials in
the last few years.

00:14:44.019 --> 00:14:45.180
I don't think you're millennials.

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:48.680
I think you're the next group, and they haven't
figured out your name yet.

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:55.750
But once they do-- and it's good not to be
a millennial, because some of the traits ascribed

00:14:55.750 --> 00:15:01.709
to them are not terribly flattering-- narcissistic,
resentful of previous generations, addicted

00:15:01.709 --> 00:15:02.709
to their iPhones.

00:15:02.709 --> 00:15:05.331
Wait, I might be a millennial, now that I
think about it.

00:15:05.331 --> 00:15:08.170
In fact, if you'll just indulge it, I forgot
to do something.

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:10.680
Hold on just a moment.

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.700
I've lost it.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:15.751
Oh, my gosh, my phone's not here.

00:15:15.751 --> 00:15:17.060
Oh, here it is.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:18.821
I forgot to do this when I came up.

00:15:18.821 --> 00:15:20.139
Would you mind, folks?

00:15:20.139 --> 00:15:23.089
Just everybody, lean in a little bit that
way.

00:15:23.089 --> 00:15:26.060
[LAUGHTER]
All right.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:28.040
Hold on.

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:31.779
How do you turn this around?

00:15:31.779 --> 00:15:33.560
Oh, I see.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:34.660
Are you ready?

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:37.079
I can't see you on that side a little bit.

00:15:37.079 --> 00:15:38.079
All right.

00:15:38.079 --> 00:15:41.209
On 3-- 1, 2 3.

00:15:41.209 --> 00:15:42.310
Thank you very much.

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:44.500
I appreciate it.

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:48.439
Of course, these generalizations about the
millennials are just that.

00:15:48.439 --> 00:15:49.439
They're generalizations.

00:15:49.439 --> 00:15:52.800
My kids are millennials, and they're pretty
good kids.

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:54.910
And of course they use their phones more than
we did.

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:59.100
If we wanted to use phones as a kid, we'd
have to carry around a five-pound device attached

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:01.449
to a wall by a cord.

00:16:01.449 --> 00:16:04.750
And if we wanted to put wallpaper on it, we'd
have to go to the wallpaper store, cut out

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:08.089
a piece of wallpaper in the shape of a phone,
and paste it to the phone.

00:16:08.089 --> 00:16:11.810
It's a laborious task, and it's very messy.

00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:14.829
As I said, there's still some debate what
to call this generation.

00:16:14.829 --> 00:16:18.399
One is post-millennial, although I find that
kind of boring.

00:16:18.399 --> 00:16:19.399
There's Generation Z.

00:16:19.399 --> 00:16:20.949
Have you heard that one?

00:16:20.949 --> 00:16:23.379
The 9/11 generation.

00:16:23.379 --> 00:16:26.810
Once they settle on naming you, they'll break
it down and tell you how you act and what

00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:29.019
you wear and how you think.

00:16:29.019 --> 00:16:34.569
I prefer to think of this group as the first
members of the Founders Academy generation.

00:16:34.569 --> 00:16:36.519
And I don't think you need to be told about
yourselves.

00:16:36.519 --> 00:16:38.529
I have the feeling you know yourselves pretty
well.

00:16:38.529 --> 00:16:44.209
I think you know how to measure your own success
and define what brings you a sense of satisfaction

00:16:44.209 --> 00:16:45.519
and accomplishment.

00:16:45.519 --> 00:16:49.640
The fundamental problem with identity politics
is that it reduces each of us.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.089
We are no longer human beings with individual
hopes and dreams.

00:16:53.089 --> 00:16:54.569
We are commodities.

00:16:54.569 --> 00:16:58.029
We are groups to be labeled and courted and
pandered to.

00:16:58.029 --> 00:17:02.750
We have no shared values of Americans, but
rather, we become a series of interest groups.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:05.940
We're told what it's thought we want to hear.

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:08.450
And we divide ourselves into these pockets
of groupthink.

00:17:08.450 --> 00:17:11.710
And we begin to speak only with others in
our group.

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:12.710
We know how we feel.

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:17.280
We begin to decide ourselves into thinking
that the only valid and true and moral way

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:23.260
to feel is the way we feel.

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:28.800
As you've learned here at Founders, the good
and the true and the beautiful are not negotiable.

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:33.380
They are not ideals that can be bent and shaped
by political or social means.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:36.120
On the Hillsdale College website, you'll find
the following.

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:39.430
"How do we measure good?

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:40.860
Is it defined by the majority?

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:44.620
Is it measured by self-satisfaction or personal
fulfillment?

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:49.480
The good of anything is found in its ability
to accomplish what it was created for, to

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:53.290
realize its purpose for existence as intended
by its maker.

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:56.540
Only in this realization can something truly
be called good.

00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:00.070
"The good is possible only in the light of
truth.

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:04.740
Not truth as it is often defined today by
personal preference or popular consensus,

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:08.470
but truth as it is, independent from opinions
and emotions.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:13.060
And where goodness and truth exist, there
you will find beauty.

00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:14.840
We were created for a purpose.

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:19.840
That purpose is not left to chance or whim,
but was determined by our maker and written

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:21.450
in our nature.

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:27.560
Our purpose is to seek truth in order to discover
and to act on what is good and beautiful in

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:29.600
this life."

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:33.400
This will always be a special class here at
Founders Academy.

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:38.080
No matter how many years pass, you 11 will
always be the first graduating class.

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:41.370
That puts you in a special place with special
responsibilities.

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:46.320
I urge you to resist those who would try to
undo the basic truths you learned here and

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:51.960
just say no to those who try to classify and
ultimately divide you into opposing identities.

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:55.400
You are not Generation Z or whatever they
want to call you.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:59.060
You are Bianca and Colton and Chase and Ashton.

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:00.500
You are Claire and Ari.

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:03.160
You are Jasmine and Ray and Emma.

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:04.300
You are Basil.

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:05.640
You are Natasha.

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:10.020
You are the first graduating class of Founders
Classical Academy.

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:11.740
I congratulate you, and I thank you.

